nd to 8 decimal places.\n\nAlso: \"Require both parseability and normalized gain at or above threshold.\" So accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nOutput accepted_call_ids: list of call_ids where accepted is True, in original order.\n\nImplementation function: solve(case) where case is a dict with keys: threshold (float), calls (list of dicts), and possibly other fields like _case_nonce but we ignore. Must not mutate case.\n\nEdge cases: Some calls may have prediction_tokens = 0? Not in fixtures. But we should handle division by zero: if prediction_tokens <= 0, what to do? Could treat normalized_gain as undefined? But spec says \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token\" implies we should ensure denominator >=1. So maybe if prediction_tokens < 1, set normalized_gain = gain (or 0?). Safer: if prediction_tokens <= 0, skip? But we can default to max(1, prediction_tokens). But in fixtures prediction_tokens are positive. I'll assume positive integers.\n\nAlso, check parseable field exists and is boolean.\n\nReturn diagnostics list in same order as calls.\n\nWe must ensure that the output matches schema exactly: accepted_call_ids list of strings, diagnostics list of objects with accepted (bool), call_id (string), normalized_gain (number). No extra keys.\n\nImplementation code must be Python source.\n\nNow produce the JSON envelope with key \"implementation\" and value as escaped Python string.\n\nI'll write solve function.\n\nLet's write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call.get('parseable', False)  # default False if missing?\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # normalize by at least one token: ensure denominator >=1\n   